³1ip³2205.231.31.86³2ts³21100667945³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= Private Property =

First private property is things, i.e., any thing in the physical universe including human bodies. Not only is it a thing, but a thing which somebody has the right to use or control and thus also to dispose of, i.e., the disposal is a type of use, final, but still a use. And the human body is where the argumentation starts.

<b>Sections:</b>

#Who owns the human body?
#Private property in things&#151;who may and does own what?
#Intellectual property&#151;does it exist?

&nbsp;

<b>Who owns the human body?</b>

Many people are confused by or put off by the use of 'own' in relation to human bodies&#151;notions of slave trade and so forth. Remember that this is predominantly an issue of use, control, and disposal.

Many consider the answer to this to be self-evident. The person who <i>is</i> the body is the obvious answer. However, the tradition of the state and those who agree with that tradition allow a plethora of 'exceptions', e.g., military conscription, mandatory jury duty, taxation, etc. For links and a discussion of more detail continue to [[Private Property and the Human Body]].

&nbsp;

<b>Private property in things&#151;who may and does own what?</b>

Again the emphasis is who may and does use, control, and dispose of things. Note as well that this relationship is a pure artifact of human thought and does not exist as an item in the physical universe. The part that does exist is the 'use, control, and disposal'. All living creatures from amoeba to human use, control, and dispose of things. The 'may' comes into play only when there is more than one human.

The Ancap position asserts the validity of the concept of private property, although highly colored by ZAP. Nobody may take, 'transfer' or confiscate private property through the violation of ZAP. That puts Ancap squarely in opposition to the state or anyone else who assumes the validity of so doing. However, there are those who, although ZAP supporters as well, assert that the concept of private property is bogus or even in itself a violation of ZAP. For links and details, see [[Private Property and Things]].

&nbsp;

<b>Intellectual property&#151;does it exist?</b>

Intellectual property is still under debate. Although there are many views on this, the crux of the matter seems to be its existence. The issue is further obscured by the individual profit to be made through the continuation of its validity as a profit source. This causes distrust in the discussion due to a possible conflict of interest.

The Existence Problem. That intellectual property (IP) is a thing and therefore also able to be property is the center here. That problem is still debated in traditional law circles. "IP is not a thing" begins the debate. What is being identified as IP only exists in the minds of humans. Thus calling it a 'thing' or 'property' is a metaphor. Traditionally, the protection only comes only with the instantiation of the thing. However to prove violation is to prove unauthorized adherence to the 'pattern'. The pattern being again the 'not physically existent'. Thus, instantiation is only used as proof of the existence and of the time of the existence of the pattern in the originator's mind. The 'pro' side asserts that is sufficient to justify and act on 'mental things' as property. The 'con' side disagrees most often in two ways:

:1. Existence as only a non-physical pattern, regardless of instantiation, is ipso facto a disability for status as property.

:2. IP does not suffer from economic scarcity. Instantiations themelves are physical things thus liable to scarcity and wholly controllable as private property. However, the pattern can be learned, copied, and independently instantiated with no loss of the pattern itself to the originator. No scarcity then entails no need for control as 'property' or otherwise.

The debate goes on. See [[Intellectual Property]].


&nbsp;


